<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho III of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1134–<a href="/years/1158">1158</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sancho the Desired</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho III (c. 1134–1158) was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1157 to 1158, the eldest son of Alfonso VII, who divided his realms between Sancho, who received Castile, and Ferdinand II, who received León. In his short reign he granted the fortress of Calatrava to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a>, leading to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>. His early death left Castile to his infant son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-castile.jpg" alt="Sancho III of Castile" width="295" height="568" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sancho_III_de_Castela_-_Compendio_de_cr%C3%B3nicas_de_reyes_(Biblioteca_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sancho was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of León and Castile</a> and Berengaria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. His nickname, the Desired (el Deseado), arose because he was the first child of his parents, born after eight years of childless marriage. In 1152 he was endowed with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najera">Nájera</a>, although, according to Carolina Carl, he never appears in documents as king of Nájera. He also succeeded Urraca the Asturian in ruling the Kingdom of Artajona. In 1151 he married Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-ramirez-of-navarre">García Ramírez of Navarre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under the will of his father, the kingdom was partitioned between his two sons: Sancho inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and his brother Ferdinand inherited León. Sancho ruled for one year, from 1157 to 1158, as king of Castile and Toledo.</p><p>During his reign the castle of Calatrava-la-Vieja was conceded to Abbot Raymond Serrat of Fitero, who proposed using the lay brothers of his monastery as knights to defend it. These knights gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>, which was confirmed in 1164 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a>.</p><p>In May 1158 the brothers agreed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a>, which outlined the spheres of conquest of Leonese and Castilian forces against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. A possible division of the Portuguese kingdom between the two sons of Alfonso VII was also considered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sancho died suddenly in the summer of 1158, shortly after the two brothers had signed their treaty, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. His death was premature, and the proposed division of the Portuguese kingdom came to nothing. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. A second child, the infante García, died at birth in 1156, which apparently also caused the death of Queen Blanche.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sancho III of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Alfonso VII died in 1157 he divided his realms again, leaving Castile to <b>Sancho III</b> and León to Ferdinand II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At Alfonso VII's death in 1157 León and Castile were separated again, Ferdinand II taking León and <b>Sancho III</b> Castile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Toledo, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=1149" aria-label="Toledo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4451 737 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sancho III of Castile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4901 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4901 1007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,-4.03&amp;year=1149">What was here in 1149? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
